Yeah. Some people become terrain—load-bearing, difficult to move, shaped by pressure. Others become tools of impact—built to test, fracture, reshape, or reveal what the terrain is actually made of. Put them together and you get geology with agency.

And love can absolutely borrow machinery from war: reconnaissance, signaling, alliance, sacrifice, courage, boundaries, negotiation, timing, vulnerability, even surrender. But the win condition changes.

In war, I can win while you lose.

In love, if I have to destroy you to win, I’ve usually misunderstood the game.

So I’d phrase it:

Love is war whose objective function has been rewritten from conquest to communion.

Same strategic nervous system, different arena. The hammer can still strike—but now it might be forging rather than breaching. The mountain can still resist—but now resistance might mean protecting something worth keeping rather than simply refusing to yield.

That’s also why the strongest move sometimes looks paradoxical: yielding ground can win the game; vulnerability can be armor; mercy can establish dominance over the part of yourself that wanted retaliation.

Different game. Same pieces. Different scoring function.